      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "-ER and -IR verbs share every ending except nosotros and vosotros: comemos and coméis, but vivimos and vivís."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "spain",
        "text": "In Spain you will hear vosotros forms all the time: ¿Habláis inglés? ¿Dónde vivís? Mind the accents on habláis and coméis."
      },
      {
        "type": "twist",
        "title": "One Spanish present, three English ones",
        "a": [
          "Trabajo en Londres.",
          "I work in London. / I'm working in London."
        ],
        "b": [
          "¿Trabajas hoy?",
          "Do you work today? / Are you working today?"
        ],
        "note": "English has three ways to say it: I work, I'm working, do you work? Spanish uses one: trabajo, trabajas. There is no \"do\" for questions either: ¿Trabajas hoy? is the same words as a statement, with your voice rising at the end."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Remember the four \"you\"s from chapter 1. One person: tú vives (informal), usted vive (polite, same form as él and ella). Several people: vosotros vivís (informal, Spain), ustedes viven (polite, same form as ellos)."
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "warn",
        "text": "Drop the pronoun. Say \"Hablo un poco de español\", not \"Yo hablo…\". Add yo or tú only for contrast: \"Yo trabajo, tú estudias.\""
      },
      {
